**Highest‑risk bug**  
`UserService.register()` calls `userRepository.existsByEmail(email)` to decide whether the e‑mail is already taken.  
The current implementation (in the real code, not the stub) queries the **whole** `users` table, including rows whose `deleted_at` column is set – i.e. soft‑deleted accounts still count as “existing”.  
Consequently a user that was soft‑deleted cannot be re‑registered, which violates the business rule *“soft‑deleted accounts should not block a new registration.”*  

The bug lives in the **data‑access layer** because the query does not filter out soft‑deleted rows. The fix must be limited to the repository implementation; we do **not** change the service or the interface.

---

## Exact changes (smallest safe fix)

### 1. `src/main/java/com/example/user/JdbcUserRepository.java`

Replace the placeholder `existsByEmail` with a real query that **excludes soft‑deleted rows**.

```java
package com.example.user;

import java.sql.*;
import java.util.Objects;

public class JdbcUserRepository implements UserRepository {

    private final DataSource dataSource;   // injected in real code (omitted in snapshot)

    public JdbcUserRepository(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = Objects.requireNonNull(dataSource);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean existsByEmail(String email) {
        String sql = "SELECT COUNT(*) > 0 " +
                     "FROM users " +
                     "WHERE email = ? AND deleted_at IS NULL";
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setString(1, email);
            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
                if (rs.next()) {
                    return rs.getBoolean(1);
                }
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to check email existence", e);
        }
        return false;
    }

    @Override
    public void insert(String email) {
        String sql = "INSERT INTO users (email, deleted_at) VALUES (?, NULL)";
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setString(1, email);
            ps.executeUpdate();
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to insert user", e);
        }
    }
}
```

